/**

\page page_tarch_tasking Tasking

\tableofcontents

The tasking backend maps dependency graphs onto OpenMP and is always built.
Callers work against the classes and functions in tarch::tasking.

# Runtime

tarch::tasking::Runtime owns the backend's process-wide state. init() caps the
OpenMP thread count to the processors available to the process and records the
result. shutdown() is a lifecycle hook. OpenMP tears itself down.

# Tasks

One unit of work is a subclass of tarch::tasking::Task, or a
tarch::tasking::FunctorTask for lambda-based work. tarch::tasking::spawnTask()
owns a task after submission.

spawnTask() accepts incoming and outgoing dependencies as token addresses. An
incoming token delays a task until the producer of that token completes.
tarch::tasking::waitForDependency() waits for one token, while
tarch::tasking::waitForAllTasks() waits for the task region.

# Synchronisation

Keep a `std::mutex` next to the data it protects and enter a cold critical
region through a scoped guard:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
{
  const std::scoped_lock lock(myMutex);
  updateProtectedState();
}
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

`std::unique_lock` with `std::defer_lock` supports conditional locking and early
release. Polling and traversal paths use tasks or atomic updates so that a
contending thread can return without serialising the process.

`tarch/accelerator/Atomic.h` offers min, max, add and increment updates through
`std::atomic_ref` with relaxed ordering. These operations are atomic but are not
guaranteed to be lock-free and do not order unrelated memory.

*/
